PROJECT AND VALUE

1. What is CivicGrid AI?

CivicGrid AI is a public infrastructure planning prototype that helps planners identify district needs and choose projects within a limited budget. It brings together citizen complaint classification, district rankings, maps, mathematical budget optimization, and policy reporting.

2. What problem are you solving?

Infrastructure departments face competing needs with limited funds. Our project makes the allocation process easier to inspect: which districts need intervention, how priorities are calculated, and why projects are funded or deferred.

3. Who are your intended users?

District planning teams, municipal bodies, and public infrastructure departments are the intended decision-makers. Citizens can contribute complaints, while planners review the resulting information and budget scenarios.

4. Explain the project in one sentence.

CivicGrid AI turns infrastructure indicators into an explainable, budget-constrained project portfolio, alongside tools for citizen reporting and policy drafting.

5. Give a practical example.

A planner enters a ₹15 crore budget. The optimizer evaluates candidate projects and selects a combination within that limit. In our local verification, it selected four projects costing ₹14.3278 crore, using the prototype’s estimated costs and beneficiary figures.

6. Is this a complaint portal or a planning platform?

Its main purpose is infrastructure planning and budget allocation. Complaint collection is a supporting capability. Currently, complaints are classified and recorded in memory; connecting them to updated district priorities is unfinished.

7. What differentiates your project?

We combine visible priority factors, geographic context, and constrained project selection in one workflow. Users can examine a district’s needs, change the available budget, and inspect which projects become funded or deferred.

8. Why use AI here?

Text classification helps structure citizen complaints, and a trained model predicts urgency for individual records. District ranking uses an explicit formula, while funding decisions use mathematical optimization. Each technique addresses a different part of the problem.

9. Why not use only a spreadsheet?

A spreadsheet could reproduce parts of the calculation. Our application adds an integrated map, complaint classification, interactive optimization, model evaluation, and report generation, making the workflow easier to explore.

10. Does the system replace government officials?

No. It is intended to support human judgment. Planners must validate inputs, review recommendations, and consider engineering, legal, environmental, and community factors before approving projects.

11. What is your strongest implemented feature?

The budget optimizer. It uses Google OR-Tools with SCIP to select a portfolio under a hard budget constraint, and we verified that it runs successfully in the local environment.

12. Is the product production-ready?

It is a functioning hackathon prototype. Production use requires verified data, persistent storage, authentication, stronger validation, corrected feature labels, and evaluation with actual planners.


ARCHITECTURE AND TECHNOLOGY

13. Explain your architecture.

The React and TypeScript frontend calls a Python Flask backend. The backend reads local datasets and district metadata, loads saved models, calculates district scores, and runs the optimizer. Results appear in maps, tables, charts, and reports.

14. What is your frontend stack?

React 19, TypeScript, Vite, and Tailwind CSS form the frontend. Leaflet provides maps, Recharts provides charts, Lucide provides icons, and jsPDF with jspdf-autotable supports PDF exports.

15. What is your backend stack?

Python Flask and Flask-CORS handle API requests. pandas and NumPy support data processing. scikit-learn, LightGBM, and XGBoost support model development, while OR-Tools handles budget optimization. Joblib stores trained model artifacts.

16. Which Google technologies do you use?

Google OR-Tools runs budget optimization locally through SCIP. The code also supports an optional Gemini API connection for policy text generation from supplied planning context.

17. Does OR-Tools need an API key?

No. OR-Tools is installed and runs locally. Gemini requires an API key.

18. Which external services are used?

The code uses Esri and OpenStreetMap map tiles, Google Fonts, and the browser Web Speech API. It supports optional Gemini requests and includes a Twilio-compatible incoming-message webhook.

19. What database do you use?

The prototype uses CSV and JSON files for datasets and metadata, saved model files for inference, and an in-memory list for recent complaints. It does not currently use a database.

20. How does the frontend communicate with the backend?

The frontend sends HTTP requests to endpoints under /api. During local development, Vite forwards those requests to Flask. A separate Server-Sent Events endpoint supplies the simulated incident ticker.

21. What are the local ports?

The frontend is configured for port 8750 and Flask for port 8749. Both bind to the local loopback interface.

22. Why use Flask?

Flask provides a straightforward way to expose Python data-processing, machine-learning, and optimization functions as APIs. It fits the prototype’s relatively small service structure.

23. Why use React and TypeScript?

React supports the interactive dashboard components, and TypeScript helps maintain consistent data structures between the interface and API responses.

24. Do you use WebSockets?

No. The incident ticker uses Server-Sent Events through the browser’s EventSource API. The current stream cycles through preset incidents.


DATA AND PREPROCESSING

25. Where does your data come from?

The repository contains a local 10,000-record infrastructure hackathon dataset and district metadata. We should describe it as prototype data because the repository does not establish verified government provenance for every field.

26. Is this live government data?

No. The current application works with local files. It does not continuously synchronize with government infrastructure systems.

27. How many districts and hotspots are included?

The dashboard contains 40 districts and serves 35 saved hotspots. This is selected prototype coverage rather than nationwide operational coverage.

28. What fields are in your dataset?

Request ID, state, district, category, subcategory, citizen upvotes, urgency level, GatiShakti status, allocated budget, days pending, and infrastructure gap score.

29. Which infrastructure sectors do you cover?

Healthcare, Water and Sanitation, Roads and Transport, Energy and Power, Education, Digital Infrastructure and DPI, and Public Safety.

30. How do you handle missing data?

The training script fills missing urgency with a default category and fills selected numeric fields using medians or defaults. For a stronger evaluation, imputation values should be learned from the training split only.

31. What feature engineering do you perform?

The script encodes categorical fields, prepares numeric inputs, assigns coordinates, and derives features such as infrastructure gap and maintenance delay. Some variables are proxies: population density is derived from upvotes, and days pending is used as a maintenance-delay proxy.

32. Are the coordinates real?

Some coordinates come from district metadata. Others are generated, and the training pipeline adds geographic jitter. Therefore, the resulting clusters should not be described as verified incident locations.

33. Are project costs and beneficiary counts verified?

No. They are formula-derived estimates in the prototype. Operational use would require validated engineering estimates, asset inventories, and beneficiary assessments.

34. How do you split the data?

The script uses an 80/20 train-test split with urgency stratification and a fixed random seed. It exports 8,000 training records and 2,000 test records.

35. Is the evaluation fully independent?

Not yet. The same test set is used to compare and select winning models. A stronger protocol would use separate training, validation, and untouched final test sets.

36. Can you reproduce the results?

The script fixes random seeds for several operations and saves model artifacts and reports. Complete reproducibility still needs tighter dependency pinning and replacement of Python hash-based coordinate generation with a stable method.


MACHINE LEARNING

37. Which model predicts urgency?

The saved priority classifier is a LightGBM pipeline. It predicts one of four urgency classes: Low, Medium, High, or Critical.

38. What accuracy did it achieve?

The saved benchmark report records 93.6% test accuracy and a macro-F1 of 0.9297 for LightGBM. These are results on the project’s dataset, not independently verified field performance.

39. Why did you select LightGBM?

The training script compares candidate models and selects the highest macro-F1, using accuracy as a secondary criterion. LightGBM narrowly outperformed XGBoost in this run.

40. Which other classifiers did you compare?

XGBoost, Random Forest, HistGradientBoosting, Extra Trees, and calibrated Logistic Regression.

41. What is macro-F1?

Macro-F1 calculates an F1 score for each class and averages them equally. It helps us examine performance across urgency classes instead of allowing a common class to dominate the overall score.

42. Why is accuracy alone insufficient?

A model can achieve high overall accuracy while performing poorly on a less frequent but important class. For this application, we should also inspect per-class recall, precision, confusion matrices, and the consequences of missed critical cases.

43. Which model predicts demand?

The saved demand model is Ridge regression. It was selected from a comparison with XGBoost, LightGBM, Random Forest, and HistGradientBoosting regressors.

44. Why did demand prediction achieve an R² close to one?

The demand target is generated from a linear formula involving upvotes, infrastructure gap, and days pending, plus noise. Ridge learns that relationship well. This demonstrates approximation of a generated target, not validated forecasting of actual future demand.

45. Is it a real 30-day forecast?

The interface presents it as projected 30-day demand, but the training target is synthetic. A credible operational forecast would require dated historical observations and evaluation against later demand.

46. Are you using BERT?

No. The current text classifier uses TF-IDF with calibrated LinearSVC, supplemented by keyword rules. BERT labels in the interface and older documentation need correction.

47. How does text classification work?

The service identifies keyword matches and can run the saved TF-IDF classifier. A matched keyword category takes precedence over the model category. Language and urgency detection also use rules.

48. Which languages are supported?

The code contains handling for English, Hindi, Bengali, Marathi, and Portuguese. This is limited script and keyword support; broad multilingual accuracy has not been established.

49. Why does NLP show 100% accuracy?

The evaluation text is generated from structured records and explicitly contains the target category. That makes the benchmark unusually easy. It does not establish 100% accuracy on naturally written citizen complaints.

50. How would you improve the NLP evaluation?

Collect independently labelled natural complaints, remove category leakage, evaluate each language separately, and include ambiguous, misspelled, mixed-language, and multi-issue submissions.

51. Is the displayed confidence always a calibrated probability?

No. The service applies confidence floors and fixed values around keyword decisions and fallbacks. Those displayed values should not be presented as independently calibrated probabilities.

52. Are there data-leakage concerns?

Yes. The generated NLP text includes the target category, some imputation happens before splitting, and model selection uses the reported test set. These need correction before making strong generalization claims.

53. Does LightGBM drive the district dashboard?

No. LightGBM predicts urgency for individual records. Main district rankings use a separate six-factor formula, and those formula-based rankings feed the budget optimizer.

54. Are your explanations actually SHAP?

No. The current waterfall uses manually calculated feature contributions. It is not a SHAP explanation of the saved classifier. We should rename it or implement actual model attribution.


PRIORITY, FAIRNESS, AND GEOGRAPHY

55. How do you calculate district priority?

The default score combines citizen demand at 30%, infrastructure gap at 25%, population-related impact at 15%, vulnerability at 10%, investment backlog at 10%, and urgency at 10%.

56. Are those inputs measured from live complaints?

No. Several factors, including complaint volume, are generated from district metadata. New complaints currently do not change the district score.

57. Who selected the weights?

They are prototype design choices. Operational weights should be reviewed with planners, justified in policy terms, and assessed through sensitivity analysis.

58. How do you address digital-access bias?

The design limits demand to 30% and normalizes it by population. This reduces dependence on raw complaint counts in the formula, but it does not prove fairness. We still need representative reporting channels and outcome-based evaluation.

59. Does the system guarantee fair allocation?

No. Fairness depends on data coverage, the scoring formula, the optimization objective, and explicit policy constraints. The current implementation has not undergone a formal fairness evaluation.

60. Could your optimizer favor larger populations?

Yes. The objective multiplies priority by estimated beneficiaries, so larger beneficiary estimates can strongly influence selection. Regional minimums or equity constraints would require explicit additions to the optimization model.

61. What does DBSCAN do?

The training pipeline clusters prepared geographic coordinates and exports hotspot summaries. DBSCAN finds dense groups without requiring a predefined number of clusters.

62. What DBSCAN settings do you use?

The training code uses standardized coordinates with eps=0.10 and min_samples=6. Those settings need validation against the spatial scale and quality of the input data.

63. Are hotspots updated immediately after a complaint?

No. The API serves saved hotspot results. Incremental or scheduled reclustering of new complaints has not been connected.

64. What does climate mode change?

It combines 75% of the base priority score with 25% of a climate composite. The current climate values are generated indicators, so this demonstrates scenario behavior rather than verified local climate risk.


BUDGET OPTIMIZATION

65. How does your optimizer work?

Each candidate project has a binary decision: fund it or defer it. The optimizer maximizes total priority-weighted beneficiaries while keeping total project cost within the available budget.

66. What is the mathematical formulation?

Maximize the sum of xᵢ × priorityᵢ × beneficiariesᵢ, subject to the sum of xᵢ × costᵢ being no greater than the budget, with each xᵢ equal to zero or one.

67. Why not fund projects in priority order?

A simple ranking does not consider how project costs interact. A lower-ranked combination may deliver a greater total objective value within the same budget. The optimizer evaluates those combinations.

68. Why use OR-Tools instead of an LLM for allocation?

OR-Tools explicitly enforces the budget constraint and solves a defined objective. An LLM is better suited to drafting explanations, while numerical allocation should remain in a verifiable calculation.

69. What does “optimal” mean?

When SCIP reports optimality, it means the best solution for the supplied candidates, objective, and constraints. It does not mean the best policy under every real-world consideration.

70. What if SCIP fails or is unavailable?

The backend uses a greedy benefit-to-cost fallback. That fallback does not guarantee optimality, and its current “optimal” label needs correction.

71. Why is some money left unallocated?

Projects are indivisible in the current model. The remaining balance may be too small to fund another available project. The system does not force spending merely to use the entire budget.

72. Can projects receive partial funding?

Not in the current optimization. Projects are selected as whole units. The interface displays spending tranches, but those do not create partial-funding decision variables.

73. Does the model include dependencies or regional quotas?

No. The implemented constraint is the total budget. Dependencies, sector minimums, regional guarantees, workforce limits, and multi-year funding constraints are future extensions.

74. Is the lifecycle model a learned forecast?

No. It is a formula-based scenario model using assumed maintenance costs, avoided losses, and a discount rate. Its outputs depend on those assumptions and need validation for actual assets.


GEMINI, REPORTS, AND VISION

75. What does Gemini do?

When configured, Gemini receives district and allocation context and generates policy text. It does not calculate the authoritative project portfolio.

76. Which Gemini model does the code call?

The request targets gemini-2.0-flash. Interface references to “Gemini 3.6 Flash” are inconsistent with the code and need correction.

77. Is this a RAG system?

It injects structured planning context into a prompt. It does not currently implement a document-retrieval system with a vector database, so “structured context grounding” is the more precise description.

78. How do you prevent hallucinations?

The prompt instructs Gemini to use the supplied context, but instructions alone do not guarantee correctness. We need output validation that checks figures, districts, and funded projects against backend results.

79. What happens if Gemini is unavailable?

The backend uses local response templates. However, some templates contain fixed figures, so those responses still require review and correction.

80. Are generated DPRs official documents?

No. They are prototype project-report drafts. They do not constitute government sanction, administrative clearance, engineering approval, or certification, even where the current template uses official-sounding language.

81. Are all report numbers tied to the selected budget?

No. Some policy and DPR templates contain hard-coded values. A required improvement is to derive every financial and beneficiary figure from validated project and portfolio data.

82. Do you analyze real satellite imagery?

The map can display real satellite basemap tiles. The separate analysis demonstration generates spectral arrays and calculates NDWI; it does not retrieve live Sentinel-2 bands.

83. What is NDWI?

NDWI is calculated as Green minus Near-Infrared divided by Green plus Near-Infrared. The demo applies this formula to generated arrays and counts pixels above a threshold.

84. Do you have a real pothole-detection model?

No. The road preset is a demonstration with generated inputs and descriptive text. A trained road-image detection model is not implemented.

85. What happens when an anomaly is injected?

The current button opens the simulator. It does not modify district scores, project candidates, or the optimized portfolio.


AUDIT, SECURITY, AND RELIABILITY

86. Is your audit ledger tamper-proof?

The backend calculates SHA-256 Merkle hashes over selected project records. This can support change detection against a trusted reference, but the prototype lacks a persistent, independently anchored ledger. “Tamper-proof” would overstate the implementation.

87. Is this blockchain?

No. It uses Merkle-tree hashing, without a distributed blockchain network or consensus mechanism.

88. Are citizen complaints permanently stored?

No. Recent complaints are stored in an in-memory list and can be lost when the process restarts. Persistent storage is necessary for a real deployment.

89. Is the live ticker genuine activity?

No. The streaming endpoint cycles through preset incidents. Actual submitted complaints appear in a separate recent-submissions queue.

90. Is SMS or WhatsApp fully integrated?

The backend can receive compatible webhook payloads and return TwiML. A live provider connection has not been verified, and the localhost configuration would need an appropriate public deployment for external webhook delivery.

91. What security is currently implemented?

The services bind to localhost, and the backend limits browser origins through CORS. These are local-development controls. Authentication, role-based authorization, durable audit records, and comprehensive input validation are not implemented.

92. Does CORS provide authentication?

No. CORS controls certain browser cross-origin requests. It does not establish who a user is or authorize their actions.

93. Can the app work offline?

Local scoring, saved-model inference, and optimization can work with installed dependencies and local files. External map tiles, fonts, Gemini, and potentially browser speech recognition require connectivity.

94. What testing have you completed?

Local checks confirmed that TypeScript validation passes, saved models load, and core district, hotspot, benchmark, prediction, and optimization endpoints respond. SCIP successfully solved the default budget scenario. Full browser, external-service, and load testing remain outstanding.

95. What known bug have you identified?

An empty complaint currently causes a server error because the submission handler expects a telemetry ID that the empty-input response does not provide. The endpoint should validate input and return a clear client error.

96. Are dependencies fully reproducible?

Not yet. Several dependencies use broad version ranges, and LightGBM is imported by the training script but missing from the requirements file. Dependency declarations and version locking need cleanup.


IMPACT, DEPLOYMENT, AND ROADMAP

97. How would you scale this?

First, introduce persistent storage, authenticated APIs, background data-processing jobs, and monitored model versions. Then measure actual workload before deciding which components need separate services or additional infrastructure.

98. What is the deployment plan?

Deploy the frontend and backend behind an appropriate HTTPS setup, configure routing and secrets, and add authentication and monitoring. The current repository is configured primarily for local demonstration.

99. What is your business model?

The repository does not establish a validated business model. A possible direction is a planner-reviewed pilot with an institution, followed by deployment and support services. That remains a proposal rather than an existing commercial arrangement.

100. Have you demonstrated real-world financial savings?

No. The current results are scenario outputs based on prototype inputs. We should not claim measured savings without a real deployment, an agreed baseline, and observed outcomes.

101. How would you measure success?

Measure planning time, agreement with independently assessed needs, ranking stability, coverage of underserved groups, and actual project outcomes. Model performance and optimizer correctness are supporting measures rather than the entire definition of success.

102. What are the most important limitations?

The biggest limitations are unverified and generated inputs, disconnected complaint-to-ranking updates, weak evaluation targets, incorrect feature labels, fixed report figures, and missing persistence and authentication.

103. What would you improve first?

First, align the interface and documentation with the implementation. Then validate the data and evaluation protocol, connect complaints to persistent planning inputs, implement genuine model explanations, and make all report figures traceable to backend results.

104. What would a real pilot look like?

Start with one planning team and a limited set of verified projects. Compare recommendations with an existing assessment process, let planners review and override decisions with recorded reasons, and evaluate usefulness before expanding coverage.

105. Why should judges select this project?

It demonstrates a concrete infrastructure-planning workflow with working optimization and saved-model inference. Its value is making priorities and budget trade-offs visible. The next step is strengthening the data and integration so the workflow can support an accountable real-world pilot.

106. What is your final takeaway?

CivicGrid AI demonstrates how transparent scoring, machine learning, and constrained optimization can work together in infrastructure planning. We have a working prototype, and we are explicit about the validation and engineering still required before operational use.